# name: test/sql/vfs_non_regular.test
# description: a reader opens a path that is not a regular file -- a pipe or a device (#120 follow-up)
# group: [sql]

require panduck

# /dev/null and /dev/stdin are POSIX. Nothing here applies on Windows, where the whole
# question does not arise.
require notwindows

#
# WHAT BROKE, AND WHY IT WAS INVISIBLE
#
# OpenFileThroughVFS (#120 step 2b) gated every reader behind `FileExists(path)`.
# FileExists() reads as "does this path exist" and does not mean that: it answers "is
# there an existing REGULAR file here", and it is FALSE for a FIFO, a character device
# and /dev/stdin -- all of which OpenFile reads without complaint, as DuckDB's own
# read_text('/dev/stdin') demonstrates.
#
# So a caller piping a document in got `file not found`, which is the ONE case the
# contract on OpenFileThroughVFS reserves that message for. The check contradicted the
# contract it was written to implement.
#
# It was invisible here because every fixture in this suite is a regular file on disk,
# and invisible to the one consumer that pipes -- duckeye routes every pandoc-mediated
# format through `read_pandoc_blocks('/dev/stdin')` -- because that project's CI had not
# been read for six days. 17 of its assertions were failing: rst, man, and every pandoc
# format at once.
#
# A character device is the hermetic way to pin this. It needs no FIFO, no second
# process and no fixture, it exists on every POSIX runner, and FileExists() is false for
# it for exactly the same reason it is false for a pipe.
#

# Before the fix this was `file not found`. It is now an open that succeeds and reads
# nothing, which is what an empty document is: zero blocks, not an error.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM read_pandoc_blocks('/dev/null');
----
0

# The same for the whole-file readers, which take the other branch of the helper
# (ReadFileThroughVFS, where a stream reporting size 0 also had to stop meaning "empty").
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM read_rtf_blocks('/dev/null');
----
0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AND THE TWO DOCUMENTED CASES STAY DISTINCT. These are the properties the gate was
# there to provide, so the fix is only correct if it keeps both of them -- a path that
# is absent still names itself missing, and a path that is present but unopenable still
# reports DuckDB's own reason rather than being collapsed into either one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

statement error
SELECT count(*) FROM read_pandoc_blocks('/nope/does_not_exist.json');
----
file not found: /nope/does_not_exist.json

# A directory exists, so it must NOT be reported as missing. DuckDB's own open error
# stands, which is the precision the helper's header claims.
statement error
SELECT count(*) FROM read_pandoc_blocks('/tmp');
----
<REGEX>:.*(Cannot open|directory|Is a directory|IO Error).*
